 "productionNotes": "The only year-by-year VR-4 count found comes from stealth316.com, an owner-run 3000GT and Stealth reference site, which derived it by scanning the vehicle database inside Mitsubishi's CAPS parts-lookup program. Its production table gives 15,539 VR-4 coupes for 1991-1999, peaking at 4,947 in 1992 and falling to 315 in 1996 and 287 in 1999, plus 877 Spyder VR-4s and 741 Spyder SLs. The author's own caveat is that the figures are believed to cover Nagoya production for Canada and the US only, and the page warns that it contains inconsistencies. A separate count, reported US sales, comes from a 1999 Mitsubishi Motor Sales of America reply reprinted on suthnr.com and labeled there as not production figures; its VR-4 rows add to 15,630, with 880 sold in 1996 and 45 in 1999, against 315 and 287 built in those years per the CAPS-derived table. Production and sales are different counts, and with no factory production total retrieved this page carries no single production total. The Spyder VR-4 count is a genuine conflict: Hagerty gives 1,034 Spyder VR-4s over two years, while the stealth316 rows give 877 (531 for 1995 and 346 for 1996); no source consulted reconciles the two. The same rows give 741 Spyder SLs separately. No Mitsubishi Motors America press release or archived US brochure could be retrieved, so there is no factory-documented US MSRP for the 1991 VR-4; two secondary figures ($30,800 and about $34,000) disagree and are carried as a dispute. Documented US prices are the 1995 Spyder VR-4 at $64,449, a MotorWeek test Spyder at $68,766, and the 1997 six-speed VR-4 coupe at $44,590. No EPA fuel economy record was fetched for this page. No Car and Driver, Road and Track or MotorTrend period test and no Mitsubishi US press material was retrieved, so US-market and period-press coverage rests on the sources cited here.",
